    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `summarize_ticket` in `app/summary.py`, and all 3 tests pass (the 2 that were there plus one new one).\n\n```python\nmessages = [SystemMessage(SUMMARY_PROMPT), HumanMessage(ticket_text(ticket))]\nreply = chat.invoke(messages, stop=[\"\\n\\n\"])\nreturn str(reply.text).strip()\n```\n\n- **One request:** it makes a single `invoke` call with the system prompt and the rendered ticket as two messages.\n- **Stop sequence:** `stop=[\"\\n\\n\"]` is a standard option that any `BaseChatModel` passes on to its provider.\n- **Plain `str`:** I used `.text` instead of `str(reply.content)`. Some chat models return their reply as a list of pieces rather than a string, and `str(content)` would turn that list into text like `[{'type': 'text', ...}]`. `.text` joins just the text parts. It returns a subclass of `str`, so I wrap it in `str(...)` to make sure a plain string comes back.\n\nI also added `tests/test_summary.py`. It uses a stand-in chat model that records what it receives. It checks that there is exactly one call with `stop == [\"\\n\\n\"]`, a system message then a user message with the right contents, and a result that is a stripped plain `str` even when the reply is a list of pieces.\n\nNo dependencies were added."
